    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Gold’s Gym is a gym chain first. Its name went onto home treadmills through a licence, and
          the machines themselves were built by ICON Health & Fitness — renamed iFIT in 2021 — alongside
          its NordicTrack, ProForm and Weslo lines. That matters for anyone searching for a Gold’s Gym
          treadmill now, whether you own an old Trainer 720, are looking at a used 430i, or saw the
          name on a listing and want to know who actually stands behind it. The honest picture in
          October 2026 is that the familiar models are discontinued, we could find no current
          licensed Gold’s Gym home treadmill line, and the questions worth asking are about parts,
          iFIT and who is selling.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Gold’s Gym Treadmills: The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Gold’s Gym treadmills were made by ICON Health & Fitness (now iFIT) under a
            licence that began in 2001, and the best-known models — the Trainer 430i and Trainer
            720 — are no longer in production.</strong> Gold’s Gym’s exclusive home-equipment deal
            with ICON expired on 31 December 2019, and on 14 October 2026 we could not find a
            current Gold’s Gym-branded home treadmill from an identifiable licensed maker.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice that means three things. If you own one, it is an ICON machine, and parts
            and manuals follow ICON model numbers rather than the gym brand. If you are offered one
            used, it is a budget-tier treadmill from a few years ago, worth a used budget-treadmill
            price. And if you see a new treadmill carrying the Gold’s Gym name, check who made it,
            who sells it and who honours the warranty before you buy, because the name alone no
            longer tells you.
          </p>
          <p>
            If what you wanted was the Gold’s Gym formula — an affordable folding treadmill sold by a
            big retailer — the same company’s ProForm and NordicTrack machines are the nearest
            equivalents, and our
            <a href="/best-proform-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm roundup</a>
            and
            <a href="/nordictrack-t-6-5-s-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack T 6.5 S review</a>
            are good places to start. If you want to avoid a subscription platform altogether, see our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-made-them" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Made Gold’s Gym Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            ICON Health & Fitness, of Logan, Utah. Several independent treadmill review sites describe
            ICON designing, manufacturing and marketing the Gold’s Gym equipment line under a
            licensing agreement dating to 2001, and ICON’s own annual report for the year to May 2004
            lists Gold’s Gym, with Reebok and Weider, among the trademarks it used “under license
            agreements with the owners of such trademarks”. ICON renamed itself iFIT Inc. in August
            2021, according to its registration statement for a planned share listing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The licence ended in 2019</h3>
          <p>
            In December 2019 Gold’s Gym issued a request for proposals from new home-equipment
            licensees, stating that its exclusive agreement with ICON expired on 31 December 2019. It
            invited bids for large and small exercise equipment across eight distribution channels,
            including supercentres and warehouse clubs, with rights to begin in early 2020. We found
            no public announcement of who, if anyone, won home-cardio rights from that process.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who owns Gold’s Gym now</h3>
          <p>
            RSG Group, the German fitness company, acquired Gold’s Gym in 2020, and its brand page
            describes nearly 600 locations on six continents, most run by franchisees. In October 2025
            Gold’s Gym appointed IMG Licensing as its exclusive global licensing agency; the
            announcement named supplements and nutrition, footwear, travel goods, sporting and leisure
            goods and apparel as the categories, and did not mention home cardio equipment.
            Gold’s Gym’s own website, when we checked, covered gyms, franchising and an online shop
            for apparel and nutrition, with no home treadmills.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where iFIT’s brands stand</h3>
          <p>
            iFIT’s 2021 registration statement lists its principal brands as iFIT, NordicTrack,
            ProForm, Freemotion, Weider, Weslo, 29029 and Sweat. Gold’s Gym is not among them, which
            fits the end of the exclusive licence in 2019. It does not tell us whether iFIT sold off
            remaining Gold’s Gym stock afterwards, and retailers did continue to list the models for a
            time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-is-sold-now" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Can You Still Buy a Gold’s Gym Treadmill New?</h2>
<p>
            Not from any source we could confirm. On 14 October 2026 a search of Amazon’s US catalogue
            for Gold’s Gym treadmills returned replacement walking belts listed as compatible with the
            Gold’s Gym Trainer 430i, and treadmills from other brands, but no complete Gold’s Gym
            treadmill. An independent review site updated in August 2024 says the Trainer 720 is no
            longer in production and that the brand does not appear to be making treadmills or other
            exercise equipment; another review, updated in October 2023, says the Trainer 430i used to
            be sold at Walmart and Amazon and is now discontinued.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What about Walmart?</h3>
          <p>
            Walmart was the store most associated with Gold’s Gym treadmills, and ICON’s 2004 annual
            report names Wal-Mart among its important customers. Walmart’s site blocked our checks, so
            we cannot tell you what it lists today. Search results show Walmart selling replacement
            parts for the Trainer 720 and 430i, such as walking belts, drive belts and safety keys,
            which is consistent with a discontinued line that owners still maintain.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Two traps in search results</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Colour filters.</strong> Retail search filters sometimes return any treadmill
            available in a “gold” finish when you search for Gold’s Gym. Check the brand field, not
            just the listing.</li>
            <li><strong>Parts listed as machines.</strong> Many “Gold’s Gym treadmill” results are
            belts, keys and motor-control parts from third-party sellers, priced well below a
            treadmill. Read the title to the end.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you do find a new treadmill carrying the Gold’s Gym name, treat it like any unfamiliar
            listing: find the manufacturer’s name in the specifications, the model number, who
            provides warranty service, and whether parts are sold. Our guide to
            <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a>
            walks through those checks for any marketplace.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Gold’s Gym Treadmill Models You Will Still Find</h2>
<p>
            Gold’s Gym models span the 2000s to the late 2010s. Treadmill Doctor’s and Treadmill
            Reviews’ indexes of Gold’s Gym models list, among others, the Trainer 315, 410, 420, 430 and 430i, the Trainer 520 and
            720, the 450, 480 and 620, the Crosswalk 570 and Maxx Crosswalk 650, the Club Trainer 690,
            the Trainer 550 and 1190, the Maxx Competitor 1080, Maxx 685T, Club Interactive 890, and
            the 1000, 1180 and 1500. Model numbers on the frame sticker begin GGTL, the ICON prefix
            for a Gold’s Gym treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Trainer 720</h3>
          <p>
            The Trainer 720 was the top of the late range and the one most searched for. Two
            independent reviews agree on a top speed of 10 mph, power incline to 10%, a 300 lb user
            limit, a 20-inch-wide belt, no Bluetooth, and a warranty of lifetime on the frame, 25
            years on the motor and one year on parts and labour. They disagree on the belt length
            (55 inches in one, 60 in the other, and 55 elsewhere in the second) and on whether its
            2.5 horsepower figure is continuous or peak, so we leave both open. It sold for roughly
            $600 to $700.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Trainer 430i</h3>
          <p>
            The 430i was the cheaper folding model with iFIT compatibility. Two reviews agree on a
            16 by 50-inch belt — small by current standards, and short for running — and on iFIT
            compatibility. The motor figure is not reliable: one review gives two different ratings
            for it on the same page. Treat it as a walking treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How they compare with what replaced them</h3>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machine</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Belt</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Speed / incline</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">User limit</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Gold’s Gym Trainer 430i</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16 x 50 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not reliably confirmed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not reliably confirmed</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Gold’s Gym Trainer 720</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 in. wide; length disputed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 mph / 10%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">300 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">NordicTrack T 6.5 S</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 mph / 10%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">300 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">ProForm 505 CST</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 mph / 10%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">325 lb</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The NordicTrack and ProForm figures are from our own verified reviews, linked below; check
            those pages for current availability. The comparison makes the point: the Trainer 720
            was essentially the same class of machine as ICON’s entry-level NordicTrack and ProForm
            folders, sold under a different name.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ifit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Do Gold’s Gym Treadmills Need iFIT?</h2>
<p>
            No. The Gold’s Gym machines that advertised iFIT, such as the 430i, were described as
            iFIT compatible: the treadmill runs in manual mode and on its built-in programmes without
            a membership, and iFIT adds trainer-led workouts and automatic speed and incline control
            if you subscribe. Reviews of the Trainer 720 say it has no Bluetooth at all, so it was
            never an iFIT machine in the current sense.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to expect on an older machine</h3>
          <p>
            Older iFIT compatibility depended on the hardware generation, and an app that worked with a
            treadmill in 2017 is not guaranteed to work with it now. If iFIT matters to you on a used
            Gold’s Gym treadmill, ask iFIT whether that model number is supported before you buy, and
            assume you will use it as a standalone treadmill if the answer is unclear. Our pages on
            <a href="/is-ifit-worth-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether iFIT is worth it</a>
            and
            <a href="/can-you-use-nordictrack-without-ifit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">using an iFIT treadmill without a membership</a>
            explain what the subscription does and does not add. We do not quote iFIT’s prices,
            because they change.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Apps other than iFIT</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill without Bluetooth can still be used with Zwift or a running app if you add a
            foot pod, which measures your pace from your shoe rather than from the treadmill. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-foot-pod-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">foot pod guide</a>
            explains the set-up and its limits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parts-and-service" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Gold’s Gym Treadmill Parts, Manuals and Service</h2>
<p>
            Because they are ICON machines, Gold’s Gym treadmills are supported the way old ICON
            treadmills are: by model number, through iFIT’s own parts service and through specialist
            suppliers. Find the model number first. It is on a sticker on the frame, usually near the
            front of the deck or the base of the upright, and begins GGTL. Photograph it with the
            serial number and quote both whenever you order a part.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where parts come from</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>iFIT.</strong> ICON is now iFIT, and its customer service remains the
            manufacturer’s route for parts on ICON-built machines. Expect older parts to run out over
            time.</li>
            <li><strong>Specialist suppliers.</strong> FitnessRepairParts.com and similar suppliers
            catalogue Gold’s Gym treadmills by GGTL model number.</li>
            <li><strong>Third-party wear parts.</strong> Walking belts, drive belts and safety keys
            for the 430i and 720 are listed by third-party sellers on Amazon and Walmart. They are
            not iFIT parts; check the model numbers they list against your sticker.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Owner’s manuals for ICON-built Gold’s Gym treadmills are published as PDF files under ICON
            model numbers, and manual archives hold many more. As with any treadmill, the manual is
            the authority on lubrication: some decks are pre-waxed and need little, others need
            silicone at set intervals. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt lubrication guide</a>
            explains how to tell, and our
            <a href="/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm troubleshooting guide</a>
            covers faults common to ICON consoles and controllers, which Gold’s Gym machines share.
            Unplug the treadmill before removing the motor hood or working on the belt.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty</h3>
          <p>
            Any warranty on a Gold’s Gym treadmill was ICON’s and ran from the original purchase. The
            long frame and motor terms quoted for the Trainer 720 were typically for the original
            owner, and its one-year parts and labour cover will have expired on any machine still in
            use. Plan on owning an unwarranted treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Recalls</h3>
          <p>
            We searched the Consumer Product Safety Commission’s database on 14 October 2026 and found
            no recall of a Gold’s Gym treadmill. There was an April 2010 recall of Gold’s Gym
            inversion systems (models GGBE0867.0 and GGCCBE0867.0), made by ICON, for a fall hazard
            from the ankle clamp or rotation strap; ICON offered a free repair kit. It does not affect
            treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="strengths-and-weaknesses" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What Gold’s Gym Treadmills Did Well, and Less Well</h2>
<p>
            Judged as what they were — mass-market budget treadmills from one of the largest home-fitness
            maker of the period — the Gold’s Gym machines had clear strengths and equally clear
            limits. These points come from the specifications above and from what more than one
            independent reviewer said; we have not used one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Did well</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Price and availability.</strong> They were cheap and easy to buy, in the stores
            where many people did their shopping, which is why so many are still around.</li>
            <li><strong>Folding and simple consoles.</strong> Most were folding machines with
            straightforward controls and built-in programmes that need no app.</li>
            <li><strong>Shared ICON parts.</strong> Because they were built on ICON platforms, wear
            parts are widely made, and technicians know the consoles and controllers.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Did less well</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li><strong>Small decks on the cheaper models.</strong> A 16 by 50-inch belt is a walking
            belt, whatever the marketing said.</li>
            <li><strong>Short parts and labour cover.</strong> One year of parts and labour, even on
            the Trainer 720, was the minimum, and reviewers noted it.</li>
            <li><strong>Motor figures that are hard to pin down.</strong> Reviews disagree on whether
            quoted ratings were continuous or peak, which makes comparison with current machines
            unreliable. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why that distinction matters.</li>
            <li><strong>A brand that no longer stands behind them.</strong> Neither Gold’s Gym nor,
            after 2019, ICON had a reason to promote the line, so support now runs through parts
            suppliers and iFIT’s general service rather than a dedicated brand.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Is a Used Gold’s Gym Treadmill Worth Buying?</h2>
<p>
            Sometimes, as a cheap walking machine. These were budget treadmills when new, and
            independent reviewers were mixed on them at the time: one long-running review site said
            it was not overly impressed with the line, another called the Trainer 720 acceptable at its
            price. Several years on, the question is less about how good they were and more about
            how much life is left.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What it is worth</h3>
          <p>
            We do not have resale data, so treat this as an estimate. A machine that sold for $400 to
            $700 new, now out of warranty and some years old, is typically worth a fraction of that:
            often under $200 for a working 430i-class machine and somewhat more for a tidy Trainer
            720, less if the belt is worn or anything is not working. Moving it is part of the cost;
            these are heavy, and the 720 in particular needs two people.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to check</h3>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Read the GGTL model number and search for parts for it before you pay.</li>
            <li>Run it for at least ten minutes across its speed range. Listen for grinding or a
            labouring motor, and watch for the belt hesitating when you push off.</li>
            <li>Run the incline fully up and down. An incline that stalls is a common fault on older
            budget treadmills.</li>
            <li>Lift the belt edge and feel the deck for wear, and check the belt for fraying.</li>
            <li>Test the safety key: pulling it must stop the belt.</li>
            <li>For the 430i, step on and walk at your normal pace. A 50-inch belt is short; if you
            feel cramped walking, you will not grow into it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            goes through these in order, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            explains why the 430i’s belt rules it out for most runners.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-buy-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. What to Buy Instead of a Gold’s Gym Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The Gold’s Gym line filled a specific gap: an affordable folding treadmill from a known
            name, sold in a mass-market store. That gap is still well served, and by the same
            manufacturer.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For the closest like-for-like.</strong> Our verified reviews of the
            <a href="/nordictrack-t-6-5-s-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack T 6.5 S</a>
            and the
            <a href="/proform-505-cst-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm 505 CST</a>
            cover two iFIT-made folders in the same class as the Trainer 720, with 55 by 20-inch
            decks, 10 mph and 10% incline. Our comparison of
            <a href="/nordictrack-vs-proform/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack and ProForm</a>
            explains how the two brands differ.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For no subscription at all.</strong> If iFIT is the part you would rather avoid,
            our roundup of
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            lists machines whose consoles do everything on their own.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>For a tighter budget.</strong> Walking pads now take much of the price point the
            430i used to occupy; our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-$300 roundup</a>
            covers what that money buys and what it does not.
          </p>
          <p>
            And if you are deciding between brands generally, our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-brands/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill brands guide</a>
            compares the companies selling treadmills now.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes Gold’s Gym treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                ICON Health & Fitness, which renamed itself iFIT in 2021 and also makes NordicTrack and ProForm. It made Gold’s Gym equipment under a licence dating to 2001. Gold’s Gym’s exclusive agreement with ICON expired on 31 December 2019, and we found no announcement of a new licensee for Gold’s Gym home treadmills.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Gold’s Gym treadmills still sold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not from any source we could confirm on 14 October 2026. Independent reviews describe the Trainer 430i and Trainer 720 as no longer in production, Gold’s Gym’s own site sells apparel and nutrition rather than equipment, and Amazon’s US catalogue showed only replacement parts. Walmart’s site blocked our checks, so we could not confirm its listings directly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a Gold’s Gym treadmill need an iFIT subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Models advertised as iFIT compatible, such as the 430i, work in manual mode and on their built-in programmes without a membership. iFIT adds trainer-led workouts and automatic speed and incline control. Reviews say the Trainer 720 has no Bluetooth, so it does not connect to iFIT. Support for older hardware is not guaranteed, so ask iFIT about your model number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the weight limit on the Gold’s Gym Trainer 720?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                300 lb, according to two independent reviews that agree on it. The same reviews agree on a 10 mph top speed, 10% incline and a 20-inch-wide belt, but disagree on the belt’s length and on whether its 2.5 horsepower motor figure is continuous or peak. Check the sticker and manual on the machine itself if those matter to you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I get Gold’s Gym treadmill parts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with the GGTL model number on the frame sticker. iFIT, which was ICON, is the manufacturer’s route for parts, and specialist suppliers such as FitnessRepairParts.com list Gold’s Gym treadmills by model number. Third-party walking belts, drive belts and safety keys for the 430i and 720 are sold on Amazon and Walmart; check their listed model numbers against yours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Gold’s Gym treadmill good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They were budget treadmills, and reviewers were mixed: one long-running site was not overly impressed with the line, another found the Trainer 720 acceptable for its price. Used, a working one can be a reasonable walking machine if it is cheap. For a new purchase, the same maker’s current ProForm and NordicTrack folders are the like-for-like choice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes With Gold’s Gym Treadmills</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Most problems come from assuming the gym brand stands behind the machine, or from paying too much for an old budget treadmill. Avoid these four.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Gold’s Gym supports the treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The gym chain licensed its name; ICON, now iFIT, made and supported the machines. A Gold’s Gym club cannot help with a fault, and any warranty was ICON’s. Contact iFIT or a parts supplier with the GGTL model number instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the name on a new listing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">With the ICON licence ended and no new home-treadmill licensee announced, a treadmill described as Gold’s Gym today could come from anyone. Find the manufacturer, the model number, who honours the warranty and whether parts exist before paying, and walk away if the listing cannot answer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 430i to run on</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 16 by 50-inch belt is short and narrow by current standards. It suits walking for most people; for running it forces a shortened stride and leaves little room for drift. If you plan to jog, look at a 55 or 60-inch deck instead.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for iFIT without checking compatibility</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Older iFIT hardware is not guaranteed to work with today’s app, and the Trainer 720 has no Bluetooth at all. If iFIT is the reason you want the machine, confirm the model is supported before you buy, and value it as a standalone treadmill otherwise.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

